WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.610
- [Narrator] The US manufacturing sector,

00:00:02.610 --> 00:00:05.330
continued it's rebound in July.

00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:06.620
Here's the way to see that,

00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:08.230
a chart showing new orders

00:00:08.230 --> 00:00:10.540
for goods that typically last three years

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:11.373
or longer.

00:00:11.373 --> 00:00:12.593
After declining sharply,

00:00:12.593 --> 00:00:14.676
following coronavirus lock downs,

00:00:14.676 --> 00:00:18.556
new orders rose to about
$230.7 billion in July.

00:00:18.556 --> 00:00:21.887
But still remain below
pre pandemic levels.

00:00:21.887 --> 00:00:23.352
Counting new orders

00:00:23.352 --> 00:00:25.320
for long lasting goods can reveal

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:27.590
whether manufacturing
activity is expanding

00:00:27.590 --> 00:00:28.910
or contracting.

00:00:28.910 --> 00:00:30.330
That can point toward changes

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:31.880
in the economy's recovery.

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:32.740
- That gives you a sense

00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:34.160
of where things are headed.

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:35.760
So it counts orders

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:37.344
that in a lot of cases,

00:00:37.344 --> 00:00:40.210
that retailers make to manufacturers.

00:00:40.210 --> 00:00:41.940
So if you're a retailer,

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:42.773
a major retailer,

00:00:42.773 --> 00:00:44.610
and you're ordering a bunch of products,

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:46.220
say a bunch of dishwashers,

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:47.690
that's because you're pretty
confident you're going

00:00:47.690 --> 00:00:49.856
to be able to sell them pretty soon.

00:00:49.856 --> 00:00:50.980
- [Narrator] The first thing

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:52.880
to know about the durable
goods indicator is

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:53.936
what it measures.

00:00:53.936 --> 00:00:55.990
The number begins with a survey

00:00:55.990 --> 00:00:58.260
of about 3,100 manufacturing companies

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:01.012
across 92 categories in the United States.

00:01:01.012 --> 00:01:02.620
The businesses report on

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:04.290
how many new orders they filled

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:06.448
to produce longer lasting durable goods.

00:01:06.448 --> 00:01:08.256
There are two types of goods.

00:01:08.256 --> 00:01:11.687
Non durables are consumed
quickly like paper,

00:01:11.687 --> 00:01:13.470
food or gasoline.

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:16.100
And then there are durables,

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:18.902
that count includes construction
materials like lumber.

00:01:18.902 --> 00:01:20.685
It also includes firearms,

00:01:20.685 --> 00:01:21.976
office chairs,

00:01:21.976 --> 00:01:24.476
tape measures and computer hard drives.

00:01:24.476 --> 00:01:27.389
It includes DSLR cameras and VR headsets,

00:01:27.389 --> 00:01:28.980
medical equipment,

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:30.860
like electrocardiography machines,

00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:33.089
or gurneys are durable goods too,

00:01:33.089 --> 00:01:35.926
as our pieces of fine jewelry and books.

00:01:35.926 --> 00:01:37.638
It also includes items

00:01:37.638 --> 00:01:41.530
that are really expensive and
aren't purchased that often.

00:01:41.530 --> 00:01:43.220
Products like heavy machinery.

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:44.100
For example,

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:45.930
these conveyor belts are durable goods.

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:48.754
Economists track the increase or decrease

00:01:48.754 --> 00:01:51.335
in the new orders on a
month to month basis.

00:01:51.335 --> 00:01:52.810
If more new orders come

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:54.370
in this month than the last,

00:01:54.370 --> 00:01:56.866
that could point toward
an economic expansion.

00:01:56.866 --> 00:01:59.150
That's because one new durable goods order

00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:00.739
can signal future activity.

00:02:00.739 --> 00:02:02.217
To see that in action,

00:02:02.217 --> 00:02:03.554
here's an example,

00:02:03.554 --> 00:02:06.442
a farm might need to purchase a tractor.

00:02:06.442 --> 00:02:08.264
So, it places an order.

00:02:08.264 --> 00:02:10.513
The tractor manufacturing company,

00:02:10.513 --> 00:02:13.730
then places orders with a
number of other companies

00:02:13.730 --> 00:02:15.560
who produce parts like engines,

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:16.961
windshields and lights.

00:02:16.961 --> 00:02:20.529
Each of those suppliers
sends orders to manufacturers

00:02:20.529 --> 00:02:23.096
for raw materials and other durable goods.

00:02:23.096 --> 00:02:25.247
The indicator can also capture

00:02:25.247 --> 00:02:26.740
the ripple effects from

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:29.130
a bigger national trend
like housing sales.

00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:31.760
- Suppose say home sales
are doing really well,

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:33.450
like it turns out they are right now.

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:34.283
It's conceivable,

00:02:34.283 --> 00:02:35.230
it's possible that a bunch

00:02:35.230 --> 00:02:38.974
of people are gonna start
buying long lasting appliances

00:02:38.974 --> 00:02:40.120
for their homes.

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:42.582
And that in turn will cause

00:02:42.582 --> 00:02:45.135
the retailers of these
long lasting appliances to,

00:02:45.135 --> 00:02:47.211
to order more from manufacturers.

00:02:47.211 --> 00:02:49.600
- [Narrator] The durable
good survey captures

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:50.839
the manufacturing activity

00:02:50.839 --> 00:02:53.134
that's connected to business activity.

00:02:53.134 --> 00:02:54.043
In this case,

00:02:54.043 --> 00:02:55.638
the purchase of a home.

00:02:55.638 --> 00:02:58.701
Many manufacturers track these new orders

00:02:58.701 --> 00:03:01.712
and send the data to the Census Bureau.

00:03:01.712 --> 00:03:04.210
Most of the manufacturers

00:03:04.210 --> 00:03:06.410
in the survey have $500 million

00:03:06.410 --> 00:03:08.151
or more in annual shipments.

00:03:08.151 --> 00:03:10.452
But it also includes some smaller firms

00:03:10.452 --> 00:03:12.868
to make the sample more representative.

00:03:12.868 --> 00:03:15.990
Economists, investors and analysts watch

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:17.180
for changes in the numbers,

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:18.532
which are released monthly.

00:03:18.532 --> 00:03:20.457
- Our main use really,

00:03:20.457 --> 00:03:23.503
is it feeds into the GDP reports which is

00:03:23.503 --> 00:03:26.410
this very obviously very
closely watched indicator.

00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:28.270
- [Narrator] Durable goods
can provide early insight

00:03:28.270 --> 00:03:29.980
into economic patterns,

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:32.008
but the indicator is not without flaws.

00:03:32.008 --> 00:03:34.523
Of the many items that
are counted in the survey,

00:03:34.523 --> 00:03:37.770
some are outweighed and can
obscure changes elsewhere

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:39.020
in manufacturing.

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:39.853
- One thing to keep

00:03:39.853 --> 00:03:41.561
in mind about the durable goods report is

00:03:41.561 --> 00:03:42.569
that it's very volatile.

00:03:42.569 --> 00:03:44.950
And the source of the
volatility are things like,

00:03:44.950 --> 00:03:45.950
like aircraft.

00:03:45.950 --> 00:03:48.370
- [Narrator] To see a better
proxy for business investments,

00:03:48.370 --> 00:03:50.360
you can look at the
non-defense capital goods,

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:51.830
excluding aircraft.

00:03:51.830 --> 00:03:54.440
It's a subsection of the
durable goods survey.

00:03:54.440 --> 00:03:55.620
- That gives you a better sense

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:56.900
of kind of,

00:03:56.900 --> 00:03:57.733
of the trend,

00:03:57.733 --> 00:03:58.953
which is really what
we're looking for here.

00:03:58.953 --> 00:04:01.110
- [Narrator] Excluding aircraft makes

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:02.500
a significant difference in the data.

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:03.845
For example,

00:04:03.845 --> 00:04:05.100
in some cases,

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:06.700
a new Boeing jet liner can cost

00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:09.680
between 50 million to over $200 million.

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:11.040
And its supply chain stretches

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:13.170
to over 12,000 companies.

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:14.003
But production

00:04:14.003 --> 00:04:16.210
of new Boeing planes has stalled following

00:04:16.210 --> 00:04:18.023
two fatal seven 37 MAX crashes

00:04:18.023 --> 00:04:20.700
and a pandemic driven
collapse in air travel.

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:23.060
500 jet orders were canceled

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:25.250
this year alone and new orders have slowed

00:04:25.250 --> 00:04:26.190
to a trickle.

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:28.980
Boeing's troubles have overshadowed

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:31.200
other manufacturing activity which skewed

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:32.947
the durable goods survey results.

00:04:32.947 --> 00:04:33.780
- [David] It made

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:35.410
the durable goods number look very strange

00:04:35.410 --> 00:04:36.360
because you had all

00:04:37.377 --> 00:04:38.610
these aircraft orders
they weren't coming in.

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:39.443
So, you know,

00:04:39.443 --> 00:04:41.810
a few major categories
like that can really skew

00:04:41.810 --> 00:04:43.660
the results.
- Public spending

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:44.970
on defense can do the same.

00:04:44.970 --> 00:04:47.226
- Things that the military
buys are also included

00:04:47.226 --> 00:04:48.059
in this report.

00:04:48.059 --> 00:04:49.920
So if the Pentagon puts in an order for

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:51.370
a bunch of fighter jets

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:53.330
or a bunch of tacks or
something like that,

00:04:53.330 --> 00:04:55.830
you know that shows up in the report.

00:04:55.830 --> 00:04:57.130
It's hugely expensive

00:04:57.130 --> 00:04:59.270
and it will really sort
of bump up the numbers

00:04:59.270 --> 00:05:00.536
for that month.

00:05:00.536 --> 00:05:01.910
But it doesn't really tell you

00:05:01.910 --> 00:05:03.320
a whole lot about the overall state

00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:04.153
of the economy,

00:05:04.153 --> 00:05:06.680
it's just the Pentagon has made
a bunch of orders for tacks.

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:08.620
- [Narrator] The durable
goods survey captures ripples

00:05:08.620 --> 00:05:11.238
of economic activity across manufacturing.

00:05:11.238 --> 00:05:13.200
But economists look beyond

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:15.370
the headline number for
a more meaningful view

00:05:15.370 --> 00:05:16.976
of where things are headed.

00:05:16.976 --> 00:05:20.143
(bright upbeat music)

